  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 142 AM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 water vapor imagery is indicating compact s/w \nenergy over the northern Great Lakes this morning, which wl dive \nsoutheast toward our cwa today. This mid lvl forcing, combined with \na pocket of enhanced 850 to 500mb moisture and developing 925mb to \n850mb northwest upslope flow wl help produce areas of showers, \nespecially this aftn. Greatest probabilities (80-95%) wl be over the \nfavorable upslope areas of the northern dacks and central/northern \nGreens btwn 18z and 00z today. Progged rh fields show deepest \nmoisture/lift near the International Border and mtns today, with \nmuch less favorable dynamics and moisture over southern VT. Did note \na weak pocket of instability over the SLV with MUCAPE of 150-300 \nJ/kg, which per the progged HRRR composite reflectivity could \nproduce a few embedded heavier convective element with maybe some \ngraupel. Snow level wl be near summit level, given 850mb temps near \n0C with minimal impacts anticipated. Highs generally range from the \nmid 30s summits to lower 50s warmer valleys under mostly cloudy \nskies. No impactful wx is anticipated tonight into Weds with temps \nhovering near normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A strong synoptic scale system is expected to produce a \nwidespread precip event acrs our cwa Thursday into Friday with some \nwet snow possible over the higher terrain, but potential \nhazards/impacts still to be determined. 00z guidance, including\nthe GFS ensemble and associated AI-GFS prog a deepening and \nclosing off full latitude trof over the Mid Atlantic/NE CONUS \nlate Thurs into Friday. Meanwhile initial sfc low tracks from \nthe Ohio Valley on Thurs morning and redevelops over the Mid \nAtlantic/SNE coast by Thurs aftn. The primary issue for snow \nlovers like myself is the lack of cold air initially, especially\nwith no arctic or modified arctic high pres located to our \nnorth/northeast. So we are going to need very high precip rates \nto help bring slightly cooler air toward the sfc and elevation \nto see accumulating snowfall, especially with bl temps btwn 2-3C\nper latest GFS sounding data at BTV. Sounding data indicates \nvery marginal thermal profiles, especially on Thursday with \nprogged 850mb temps hovering near 0C and 925mb 0 to 1C, which \nsupports snow levels above 3000 feet and minimal impacts. \nHowever, as system pulls away, winds shift to the northwest and \nllvl caa develops on backside, allowing thermal profiles to cool\nand support some accumulating snowfall. The challenge is how \nquickly does the cooler air advect on backside and does moisture\nlinger around to support accumulation snowfall. At this time, \nthe highest probability (60-80%) of several inches of \naccumulating snowfall would be acrs the Dacks and Green mtns of \nVT, Thurs night thru Friday. GFS is very slow with lifting \nmid/upper lvl circulation eastward with sfc weakness/invert trof\nlingering into Saturday. This idea would support additional \nsnowfall possible, especially trrn. For now have high \nprobability of precip (55-80%) for most of our cwa Thurs into \nFriday with some (1-3\") wet snow accumulation acrs the High \nPeaks and Spine of the Green Mountains. Temps wl be trending at \nor slightly below normal by Friday with highs upper 20s summits \nto lower 40s valleys and lows mid 20s to mid 30s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Upper level troughing will prevail for much of the \nweekend, leading to a mostly cloudy and cool stretch. Upslope \nshowers are likely Saturday, potentially lingering part of the way \ninto Sunday. However, any organized synoptic precipitation will be \ndone. Snow levels currently look to be somewhere around 1,000 ft, \nbut they could make it all the way down into the Champlain Valley \nduring heavier showers or at night when it is cooler. Several \ninches will be possible in the mountains, but anything in the \nvalleys would likely be lighter and have a hard time accumulating. \nNBM probabilities of 3+ inches are in the 20-40 percent range in the \nmountains, but these are likely underdone as its long range \ncomponents are too coarse to fully depict terrain effects and \nrelevant static stability. Heading into next week, a large surface \nhigh pressure looks to build into the region, bringing increasing \nsunshine and slightly moderating temperatures.",
